Day 272/300: Recover Binary Search Tree

Problem Statement #99

Problem Statement Explantion:

You are given the root of a binary search tree (BST), where the values of exactly two nodes of the tree were swapped by mistake. 
Recover the tree without changing its structure.

Solution Approach:

In a valid Binary Search Tree, an in-order traversal visits nodes in a strictly increasing order. 
If two nodes are swapped, the sorted sequence will have one or two "disruptions" where a larger value appears before a smaller value.

Algorithm Steps
Traverse: Perform an in-order traversal (Left -> Root -> Right).
Identify: Keep track of the prev node visited.
First Violation: The first time prev.val > root.val, the prev node is one of the swapped nodes.
Second Violation: The last time prev.val > root.val, the root node is the other swapped node.
Swap: After the traversal, swap the values of the two identified nodes.

Here's the code:

class Solution {
    TreeNode first = null;
    TreeNode second = null;
    TreeNode prev = new TreeNode(Integer.MIN_VALUE);

    public void recoverTree(TreeNode root) {
        traverse(root);
        int temp = first.val;
        first.val = second.val;
        second.val = temp;
    }

    private void traverse(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return;
        
        traverse(root.left);
        
        if (first == null && prev.val > root.val) {
            first = prev;
        }
        if (first != null && prev.val > root.val) {
            second = root;
        }
        prev = root;
        
        traverse(root.right);
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(N)
Space Complexity: O(H)



